“We use the Strategy pattern when we have multiple ways of performing the same operation and we want to switch the behavior at runtime without using if-else conditions.
“We use the Observer pattern when a change in one object should automatically notify and update multiple dependent objects.”
We use Singleton when only one instance is required across the application.
We use Builder when an object has many parameters and needs validation while maintaining immutability.
We use Prototype when object creation is costly and cloning improves performance.
We use Factory when we want to hide object creation logic and achieve loose coupling by programming to interfaces.
“We use the Adapter pattern when we want to make an existing class work with a client without modifying its code by mapping its methods to the interface expected by the client.”
“We use the Decorator pattern when we want to add new behavior or responsibilities to an object dynamically at runtime without modifying its existing code.”
